NATO the North Atlantic Treaty  Organization was set up in 1949 it began  as a defensive alliance between America  and Western European powers to guard  against the Soviet Union and its  communist satellites involved in a tense  rivalry with the West after the Second  World War it is now under pressure  particularly from Donald Trump in the  United States who thinks that his  European partners don't carry their fair  share of the burden and is wanting them  to spend a lot more money on defense and  there are various fundamental reasons  why Europe and America are not quite the  same soul mates as there used to be time  was a hundred years ago when almost all  of the immigrants in America or the  foreign-born population were from Europe  now only 10% are America's trade is  getting pulled off towards China and  China itself is the rising rival  superpower so America's attention is  increasingly drawn towards the Pacific  rather than across the Atlantic I think  it's important to say that NATO has been  extraordinarily successful it's often  called the most successful alliance in  history the average lifespan over the  past 500 years for a collective defense  alliance is just 15 years and NATO is 70  so it's it's a great success story  although it's true that Asia is rising  in particular and also other emerging  markets are becoming more important the  relationship between Europe and America  remains a terribly strong one 3 billion  dollars worth of trade every day lots of  shared interest shared values sometimes  wobbly at the edges but nevertheless  fundamentally a shared belief in  democracy which is something that can't  be said for for example China and Russia  and more authoritarian regimes that  America and its European partners  increasingly confront in order to stay  relevant for the future NATO needs to do  a number of things first of all it needs  to live up to the things that it's  already promised to do and back in 2014  NATO members promised that they would  aim to spend 2% of GDP on defense a lot  of countries are woefully short of that  so that's task number one the Europeans  in particular need to get much more  serious about plugging gaps in their own  defence capabilities but perhaps the  biggest task is the third one responding  to the rise of China a new world where  geopolitics is going to look very  different and NATO needs to really work  towards a new sort of partnership  between Europeans and Americans that can  really take into account a response to  China as a rival Challenger increasingly  in the future  [Music]  really the danger for NATO is that if it  doesn't respond vigorously enough  America in particular will go its own  way will get fed up will decide as  president Trump has already threatened  to do that it will just do its own thing  and walk away that would be very  worrying for many Americans but  particularly for the European allies if  NATO plays its cards right I think it  will continue to have a critical role in  the peace and prosperity for the coming  decades and that's the hope but it  should be very firmly setting its sights  on the future as it celebrates its 70th  birthday rather than indulging in  nostalgia for the past
 

